MITRE ATT@CK Journey

Tactic 11: Collection


🎯 Goal:
The adversary is trying to collect valuable data before stealing it.

Think of it this way:
"I've broken into the network. Now let me gather the important files."


What attackers collect:

Password databases
Documents (.docx, .pdf)
Excel files
Customer databases
Financial records
Browser data
Screenshots
Clipboard contents
Emails
SSH keys
Source code


Common MITRE Techniques:

Data from Local System
Data from Network Shared Drive
Screen Capture
Clipboard Data
Audio Capture
Video Capture
Automated Collection


Windows Event IDs to Investigate:
Collection doesn't have one dedicated Event ID, but SOC analysts commonly correlate:

4688

Process Creation
Check if tools like:
powershell.exe
cmd.exe
robocopy.exe
xcopy.exe
7z.exe
rar.exe
are being used to gather files.


4663

Object Access
Detects access to sensitive files or folders (when Object Access auditing is enabled).

5145

Network Share Access
Indicates files being accessed over SMB shares.


What should a SOC analyst ask?

Whenever you observe file collection activity, investigate:

✔️ Who accessed the files?
✔️ What files were collected?
✔️ When did it happen?
✔️ Where were the files located?
✔️ Why is the user accessing them?
✔️ How many files were accessed?
✔️ Was the data compressed?
ZIP
RAR
7z
✔️ Was PowerShell used?
✔️ Was the activity outside normal working hours?
✔️ Did the same host later upload or transfer the data?


Real-world example

An attacker gains access to an HR employee's laptop.

They execute:

robocopy C:\Users\HR\Documents C:\Temp\Collected /E

Then they compress everything:

7z a HR_Data.7z C:\Temp\Collected

Next, they prepare to send that archive outside the organization (the next tactic covers that).


SOC Investigation Flow:

User Login
      ↓
4688 (robocopy.exe)
      ↓
4663 (Sensitive files accessed)
      ↓
7z.exe launched
      ↓
Archive created
      ↓
Look for network activity



